2186358167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360298 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab4151e7-48c9-5220-81f5-230568253159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360826 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360826 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360826 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360826 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $X∼B(3,0.3),$ compute $P(X\\ge 2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32727390-885c-5602-b623-b9ec09406443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360826 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3})(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{3-3} \\\\  & =3(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+1(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{0} \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361389 #edb/ref 1080 "0.9547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361389 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361389 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7ce615c1-c056-50fa-a609-0af78e4d7278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361477 #edb/ref 1080 "0.9078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361477 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361477 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "90bb771b-acb1-5d7b-82d6-e6cb2a2f76c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361607 #edb/ref 1080 "0.9625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361607 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361607 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ae2222aa-6db4-5123-82d5-43e005e9d05a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361894 #edb/ref 1080 "0.9922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361894 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361894 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eddad30f-5a3e-5f84-ac5e-0779efe69930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361943 #edb/ref 1080 "0.9375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361943 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361943 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f6f90992-a3f4-5ce7-bb9a-05f52ebcdf90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362154 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bd6feca6-76ee-5295-b8ca-70efe4a6b0da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362210 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362210 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362210 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362210 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $X∼B(7,0.5),$ compute $P(X\\le 6).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd0fa54f-0d49-5cf3-b205-811b4116ac34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362210 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(X\\in \\{7\\}) \\\\  & =f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{7-7} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.0078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0078 \\\\  & =0.9922\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
